# jemdoc: menu{MENU}{mpc.html}
= Model Predictive Control

/Published February !!!, 2026/

/The [cem.html Cross-Entropy Method note] is optional but recommended background reading./

here is the canonical RL representation figure

take a control model
optimize over the control input by planning over a short time horizon
determine immediate next action based on that optimization
once that action is applied, you re-optimize at the next step
and the process continues

$K(x_k) = u_{k+1}(x_k)$ where $u_{k+1}(x_k)$ is the optimal short time control starting at the initial condition $x_k$. the $k+1$ in $u_{k+1}$ means that we are only implementing the first time step.

- To select an action, MPC searches for an optimal action sequence under the learned model and executes the first action of that sequence, discarding the remaining actions. Typically this search is repeated after every step in the real environment, to account for any prediction errors by the model and to get feedback from the environment.

why powerful:
- easy to impose constraints — on both the states and the action so never violate SLAs, etc.
- both hard and soft constraints (define these)
- because re-do optimal contorl at each time step, less risk if system does nto behave locally as the model expects
- gradient-based methods (such as in RL) can converge to suboptimal local optima and are prone to exploding or vanishing gradients

difference from RL:
- there is some setpoint or goal you want to reach
- very expensive (full optimization at each timestep) — relies on fast hardware



In MPC, there are two “loops,” and CEM only lives in the **inner** one:

1) Inner loop: CEM optimization at the current state (s_t)

Yes — in practice, **CEM stops after a fixed budget**, because MPC has a real-time deadline. Typical stopping rules are:

* **Fixed number of CEM iterations** (T) (most common)
* **Fixed number of samples** per iteration (N) (so total rollouts (=N\times T))
* **Time budget** (stop when you hit, say, 10–50 ms)
* Optional early stopping:

  * best-so-far objective improvement below a tolerance
  * covariance “collapse” (e.g., (\mathrm{tr}(\Sigma)) below a threshold)
  * elite set degeneracy / ill-conditioning triggers fallback

At the end of that inner solve, MPC applies either:

* the **first action** of the best sequence (receding horizon), or
* the **mean** of the final sampling distribution (common in MPPI-like variants),
  then executes just that first action.

2) Outer loop: MPC receding-horizon execution over time

MPC itself repeats at every control step:

s_t ;\rightarrow; \text{(run CEM for a bit)} ;\rightarrow; a_t ;\rightarrow; s_{t+1} ;\rightarrow; \cdots

So MPC doesn’t “finish” until the episode/task ends, but **each CEM call is intentionally truncated**.

### Practical detail you’ll see almost everywhere

To make truncation work well, MPC+CEM usually **warm-starts** the next timestep by:

* shifting the previous best action sequence forward by one step, and/or
* initializing ((\mu,\Sigma)) from the previous iteration’s distribution (shifted in time),
  sometimes with extra variance injected.

So: **CEM in MPC almost always stops after a set number of iterations/samples or a time limit**, and MPC keeps running by re-solving from the new state each step.


